<GuideLayout
  title="Choosing your first hiking tent"
  description="A beginner framework for balancing weather protection, internal space, carried weight and ease of setup."
  eyebrow="Shelter"
  readTime="8 min read"
>
  <h2>Choose for your real trips</h2><p>
    A first tent should suit the places you expect to visit most often. A mild,
    sheltered overnight asks something different of a shelter than exposed
    alpine country or persistent Tasmanian rain.
  </p>
  <h2>Freestanding or trekking-pole shelter?</h2><p>
    Freestanding and semi-freestanding tents are often intuitive to pitch and
    easier to move before pegging down. Trekking-pole shelters can reduce
    carried weight but may demand more careful site selection, tension and
    pitching practice.
  </p>
  <h2>Check usable space, not just capacity</h2><p>
    “Two person” can mean two narrow mats with little room left over. Compare
    floor dimensions, wall angle, peak height and vestibule space. A two-person
    tent is often a comfortable first choice for one hiker who accepts the
    weight.
  </p>
  <h2>Weight includes more than the tent body</h2><p>
    Published minimum weights may omit pegs, stuff sacks, repair sleeves or
    optional groundsheets. Compare the complete weight you will actually carry.
  </p>
  <h2>Pitch it before the trip</h2><p>
    Learn the sequence at home, check that every part is present and practise
    getting the fly tension right. If the shelter is not fully freestanding,
    practise on soil similar to your expected campsite.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
